raight?\nWhat about 3-4-5-6-7? No. Too many singles.\n\nSo let me check the hand:\nPair 3, Pair 4, Pair 5 (Pair Straight)\nPair 7, Pair 9, Triplet 10, Pair J, singles: 6, 8, Q, 2, RJ.\n\nIf I play the Triple 10 with a pair, it may work. But if I use the 3s and get 44-55, that will not work.\n\nThe move: 33-44-55, what does it leave me with?\nIt leaves me with a set of isolated cards, but there is potential to build another straight!\n\nMy plan:\n1.  Play 33-44-55 (Clears 6 low cards).\n    Hand left: 6, 77, 8, 99, TTT, JJ, Q, 2, RJ\n    Look at the rest.\n    I see a straight: 6-7-8-9-10-J-Q available.\n    I can now create a straight or start with the Triplet.\n\n    If I use the straight:\n    Leaves: 7, 9, 10, 10, J, 2, RJ.\n    I can control RJ and the 2.\n\n    If 33-44\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: The hand features a solid structure of pairs and a potential long straight. The two primary options are playing the long straight (3-4-5-6-7-8-9-10-J-Q) or the sequence of pairs (33-44-55). Playing the long straight removes 10 cards but leaves a very weak residue of unconnected low singles (3, 4, 5, 7, 9, J) and a pair of 10s, which is difficult to win with. Playing the sequence of pairs (33-44-55) effectively clears 6 low cards while preserving a strong mid-range structure (6, 77, 8, 99, 101010, JJ, Q, 2, RJ). This remaining hand can form a straight (6-Q) or play as pairs/triplets, and is protected by the high cards (2, Red Joker).\n\nReason: The sequence of pairs (33-44-55) is the most efficient opener. It removes the lowest cards in the hand in a cohesive block. The alternative of playing the 10-card straight destroys the hand\'s structure, leaving too many single losers. By playing the pairs first, we retain the option to play a 6-Q straight later or adapt based on opponents\' responses, while holding the Red Joker and 2 for control.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♥3", "♦3", "♣4", "♦4", "♣5", "♦5"]}'}
