nd the probability of me winning.\n\nSixteenth Thought: The problem is that the singles can be beaten by a higher single.\n\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The hand consists of several pairs (77, 99, JJ), a triple (10-10-10), and loose singles (6, 8, Q, 2, Red Joker). The 7-card straight (6-7-8-9-10-J-Q) is the most powerful opening. Because the player holds three 10s, there is only one 10 left in the opponents\' hands. Any straight higher than 6-Q must include a 10 (e.g., 7-K, 8-A), making it statistically very unlikely for the opponents to beat this move. This move efficiently clears the weak singles (6, 8, Q) and significantly reduces the hand size while retaining high control cards (2, Red Joker) for the endgame.\n\nReason: This move clears 7 cards at once, including difficult low singles (6, 8). The remaining hand (7, 9, 10-10, J, 2, Red Joker) is manageable given the two high control cards. The alternative of playing a Triple with Pair (10-10-10-7-7) is weak because opponents likely hold higher triples (Q, K, A) and can easily gain control.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♥6", "♦7", "♣8", "♣9", "♦10", "♥J", "♦Q"]}'}
